  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "eyy8lp3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRICITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current (I) is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge (Q = I × t).",
        "Ohm's Law states that V = IR under constant physical conditions such as temperature.",
        "Resistivity (ρ) depends strictly on the material composition and temperature, not on length or area.",
        "In series connections, electric current remains uniform; in parallel connections, voltage remains uniform.",
        "Joule's Law of Heating specifies heat produced as H = I²Rt."
      ],
      "id": "v7kzwq4"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Current",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Commercial Unit (1 kWh)",
          "value": "3.6 × 10⁶ J",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Fuse Wire Trait",
          "value": "Low MP, High R",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Resistor Colour Bands",
          "value": "BBROYGBVGW",
          "icon": "book"
        }
      ],
      "id": "upbf1x8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "13rs1o1",
      "content": "Electricity is one of the most important and high-weightage topics in RRB examinations. This chapter covers electric charge, current, potential difference, resistance, Ohm’s law, electric power, heating effect of current and related numericals."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3jmjch",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. ELECTRIC CHARGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y4or1ox",
      "content": "• Charge is the basic property of matter responsible for electrical phenomena."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tfr31qo",
      "content": "• SI unit: Coulomb (C)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4qqps3g",
      "content": "• 1 Coulomb = 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dwz6083",
      "content": "• Charge is conserved and quantized."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2mwyszc",
      "content": "• e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C"
    },
    {
      "id": "8sveucu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3hgqoc",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "7t36t9h",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_7t36t9h.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a4zolgr",
      "content": "• Electric current is the rate of flow of charge.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hl4n9s9",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "9nm103t",
      "content": "SI unit: Ampere (A)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yx0kd7r",
      "content": "1 Ampere = 1 Coulomb/second"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9ntjtaf",
      "content": "Conventional current flows from positive to negative terminal."
    },
    {
      "id": "u0wthc7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6pjqmkf",
      "content": "• Work done in moving unit charge between two points."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k2cfoyj",
      "content": "Formula: V = W / Q"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i1nemm3",
      "content": "SI unit: Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g0xumnj",
      "content": "1 Volt = 1 Joule/Coulomb"
    },
    {
      "id": "29ltnuk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. OHM’S LAW"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "elndrf5",
      "content": "At constant temperature, current is directly proportional to potential difference."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "z7uk5t8",
      "content": "V ∝ I"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8cj20tf",
      "content": "V = IR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "inrl6zv",
      "content": "Graph of V vs I is a straight line."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Ohm’s law states that the current through a conductor between two points is directly proportional to the voltage across the two points.",
      "id": "tbdzgk2"
    },
    {
      "id": "xt915hh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. RESISTANCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4283f7e",
      "content": "• Opposition offered by conductor to flow of current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cyvcdjg",
      "content": "Formula: R = V / I"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i2yhh27",
      "content": "SI unit: Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tkholwu",
      "content": "1 Ohm = 1 Volt/Ampere"
    },
    {
      "id": "0brw9vw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. FACTORS AFFECTING RESISTANCE"
    },
    {
      "id": "zp8230s",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Factor</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Relation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Length (L)</p>",
            "<p>R ∝ L</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Area (A)</p>",
            "<p>R ∝ 1/A</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Material</p>",
            "<p>Depends on resistivity</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Temperature</p>",
            "<p>Increases for metals</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dq2dihv",
      "content": "Formula: R = ρL / A"
    },
    {
      "id": "7vu2th6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. RESISTIVITY (ρ)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9lcyn91",
      "content": "• Property of material."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h9fgd6n",
      "content": "SI unit: Ohm metre (Ωm)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xjny1hj",
      "content": "Depends on temperature and material only."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Series Combination",
        "Parallel Combination"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Electric Current (I)",
          "Same through all resistors",
          "Divides among individual branches"
        ],
        [
          "Potential Difference (V)",
          "Divides across each resistor",
          "Same across all resistors"
        ],
        [
          "Equivalent Resistance (Req)",
          "Req = R1 + R2 + R3 (Increases)",
          "1/Req = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 (Decreases)"
        ],
        [
          "Circuit Fault Impact",
          "One component fails, whole circuit breaks",
          "One component fails, other branches function normally"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "hljj5l3"
    },
    {
      "id": "xxwf5yt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. COMBINATION OF RESISTORS"
    },
    {
      "id": "ee1dxct",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "o7w344e",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Combination</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Formula</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Current/Voltage</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
